{"target":"src/hivemind/prompts/collision-v4.txt; poc/data/collision-v4-probe-2026-11-05/results.json","class":"Coarse and fine answers merged","severity":"blocker","evidence":"The prompt says a coarser or finer reading of one outcome is one question. In probe pair fed_coarse_fine, draw 1, the v3 reader explains that an unchanged rate and a rate cut both answer the hike question “no” but produce different target ranges; the probe nevertheless labels the pair expected “same”.","why":"D248 and standard §4 require separately meaningful answers that can move apart to remain separate. A threshold answer can lose information needed to resolve the full numerical question, so treating every coarse/fine pair as identical can erase a separately actionable question.","what_would_settle_it":"Replay stored matching with a blinded set containing both genuinely equivalent wordings and threshold-versus-full-range questions; inspect which questions and evidence the real writer retains."}
{"target":"docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/split-a-v2-prompt.txt; src/hivemind/prompts/collision-v4.txt","class":"Deal stages collapsed too broadly","severity":"serious","evidence":"A says “One deal or one programme is one question: its agreement, approval and closing are steps of it”; collision-v4 gives agreement, approval and closing as the same-question example. The probe labels hf_completion_vs_agreement “same”.","why":"Standard §4 and D248 expressly preserve a pending deal’s signing question separately from a programme’s amount question. The blanket sentence omits that boundary and can suppress a separately permitted question; four sends on the acquisition do not test the exception.","what_would_settle_it":"Use a stored or independently constructed signing-versus-programme-amount pair and check both A’s proposal and the actual matching and writer outcome against D248."}
{"target":"docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:247; traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1250.json","class":"Shared-quantity identity overreach","severity":"serious","evidence":"At seq 1250, the candidate asks for five hyperscalers’ total capital expenditure; v5 asks for four hyperscalers’ data-center capital expenditure. Record preparation assigns spend_total and the code merges to v5 by key without an analyst review. D197 limits automatic identity to candidates admitted as shared_quantity with a quantity measure; the split schema supplies only a broad shared_measure classification.","why":"The two scopes differ in both population and what spending is counted. Treating every theme quantity assigned the same broad measure as the same aggregate assumes the disputed identity before the analyst can decide it; the one demonstrated merge cannot establish that all such pairs are equivalent.","what_would_settle_it":"Replay the stored keyed candidates against independently labelled same-theme, same-year pairs containing both alternate estimates of one aggregate and independently meaningful spending quantities; count incorrect merges and missed merges."}
{"target":"docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:241; traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1207.json","class":"Year selected from unrelated text","severity":"serious","evidence":"Seq 1207 was dropped because metadata gave “2028” for an FY2028 question. The subsequent question_year change returns the first fiscal year anywhere in the question. A read-only call returns FY2025 for “What will the calendar 2028 market size be versus FY2025?” with prepared year 2028.","why":"A comparison year is not necessarily the period being predicted. The repair fixes the observed wording but can make the horizon check use an old comparison period and admit a future annual question outside the permitted horizon.","what_would_settle_it":"Run the existing handover check on questions with a target year and a different comparison fiscal year in both orders, including a beyond-horizon target; verify the chosen period and resulting admission."}
{"target":"docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:294; test_split_v2.py","class":"Horizon check bypassed without ladder","severity":"serious","evidence":"The code sets figure only when rungs exist and have numeric bounds, then applies beyond_next_year only if figure is true. A read-only call to the test’s handover helper with “What share will NVIDIA hold in 2028?”, year “2028”, and an empty ladder returns one candidate and no drop; the same test checks the question only with NUMERIC rungs.","why":"The 2026 article can therefore open a 2028 calendar-year figure simply because its ladder is missing. Optional ladder admission does not authorize bypassing the separate year limit in standard §4.","what_would_settle_it":"Add the empty-ladder version of the existing 2028 case to the stored-data replay and check whether the question reaches the writer."}
{"target":"docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:153; test_split_v2.py","class":"Dash can change a number's sign","severity":"serious","evidence":"compare_form translates every em dash to a space. A read-only call accepts model quote “Profit rose $10 million.” against source “Profit rose —$10 million.” and returns the latter; the existing test only checks that changing 106% to 160% is refused.","why":"An em dash can be punctuation, but in a numerical context the transformation can also make a positive model statement match a negative source figure. Returning exact source characters does not repair the separately stored summary.","what_would_settle_it":"Run positive and negative controls for dashes used as punctuation and as signs, and inspect both the returned quote and its stored summary."}
{"target":"docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:188; traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1243.json","class":"Quote repair leaves unsupported summary","severity":"serious","evidence":"At seq 1243, B’s quote includes the later unnamed-source account that acquisition was part of ongoing talks and that the deal had not been announced. The handover keeps only “Now, according to reports, the artificial intelligence tech giant Nvidia is slated to buy Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.” Its one_line still asserts ongoing talks and no announcement, and original_who still names the later anonymous source.","why":"The kept passage no longer supports the whole stored summary or attribution. D266 permits keeping an exact copied sentence that runs on; it does not establish that claims from the discarded continuation remain supported by that sentence.","what_would_settle_it":"Replay all run-on repairs and compare each stored summary, speaker, conditions and date against the exact passage retained; count unsupported fields."}
{"target":"docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:124; poc/data/split-v2-followups-2026-11-05/results.json","class":"Annualized reference can use the wrong figure","severity":"serious","evidence":"The prompt says that when the source gives only part of a year, “the full-year pace that figure implies is the reference.” In the follow-up probe, seq 1246 receives a neutral range around $10 billion from doubling trailing revenue. The production plan identifies the relevant guided figure as $20 billion, itself expected to more than double.","why":"A mechanically plausible ladder can anchor judgement to a weaker or different base even when a more relevant forecast exists. Passing check_ladder and check_bounds establishes shape, not that the comparison is the right one.","what_would_settle_it":"Read all six part-period probe outputs against the complete source and any available full-year guidance; replay the affected stored judgement with the recorded and independently checked references."}
{"target":"traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1251.json; split-v2-design.md §6","class":"Question cannot be resolved as written","severity":"serious","evidence":"Seq 1251 writes “What share of NVIDIA’s FY2028 revenue will come from customers it has financed in some way?” and says to check the annual report for that share. The design says this derived share, which no public material reports, opens in every run.","why":"A sourced estimate is not proof that the future outcome will be publicly disclosed in the required form. The accepted five-rung shape does not prove that the question can be resolved, as required by standard §4.","what_would_settle_it":"Inspect the source and actual public reporting basis for this and the other opened questions, and have an independent reader identify the material that could settle each outcome."}
{"target":"traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1207.json; split-v2-design.md §6","class":"Existing-question filing remains noisy","severity":"serious","evidence":"At seq 1207, B files the proposed launch of futures contracts and an explanation of what a futures curve might show under v2, the FY2027 total-revenue question. The design reports 16 helpful out of 30 sampled existing-question passages in confirmation, down from 21–23 of 30 in the stopped-run readings.","why":"The futures-launch statement does not itself establish a demand, supply, competition or pricing effect on the FY2027 revenue answer. The sample exposes a live filing problem, but does not establish the full error rate or its downstream effect.","what_would_settle_it":"Blindly read a preselected set of complete source articles, B outputs and final written links, including omissions, then inspect the affected judgement inputs."}
{"target":"traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1213.json; split-v2-design.md §§3, 6","class":"Accepted ladders are not judgement coverage","severity":"serious","evidence":"Seq 1213 writes the FY2028 total-revenue question with rungs [] and judgements []. In the confirmation traces, 22 questions were written, six have no ladder and only 14 have a recorded judgement during the observed window. The design reports 15 of 21 numeric questions with an accepted ladder.","why":"A missing ladder is permitted by the later incomplete-details decision, but judging.py:301 excludes it from judgement. A ladder’s structural acceptance and a judgement’s existence also do not show the judgement is correct.","what_would_settle_it":"List every written question, its ladder, dates and observed judgements; compare the judgement reasons with source evidence and the frozen judgement ruler where that ruler applies."}
{"target":"traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1237.json; split_seven_day_poc.py:207","class":"No usable check date recorded","severity":"serious","evidence":"Seq 1237 opens a question about the mid-September Federal Reserve meeting with due_day empty. Across the 22 confirmation-run questions, all 22 written due_day values are empty. explicit_day accepts only a complete calendar date, while record preparation is told not to invent one.","why":"Leaving an unavailable exact date empty follows the later incomplete-details decision, but the run does not demonstrate how these questions will later acquire a usable check date or be closed. The number of successfully opened questions is therefore not evidence that their full lifecycle works.","what_would_settle_it":"On a copied ledger, trace an undated question through later information, a date update and eventual resolution using the actual production path; report which steps exist."}
{"target":"docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/blind-questions-confirm-2026-11-05/prompt-1.txt; split-v2-design.md §6","class":"Blind question review cannot measure recall","severity":"serious","evidence":"The packet asks the reader to judge each emitted question using its question text, article header and opening passages; it does not ask the reader to read every complete article and identify questions that were never proposed. The design reports 3 of 22 confirmation questions should not open.","why":"This is a model reader’s assessment of the questions produced, not a measurement of important questions missed. Opening passages selected by the system also cannot independently establish everything the complete article supports.","what_would_settle_it":"Before reading A’s answers, have an independent reader inspect complete preselected articles and the prior library, list important new questions, then compare those lists with A’s output."}
{"target":"docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/blind-evidence-confirm-2026-11-05/prompt-1.txt; split-v2-design.md §§4, 6","class":"Passage sample measures precision only","severity":"serious","evidence":"The reader is given sampled returned quotes, summaries, titles and filed questions. The reported opening-passage result is 18 of 30, and the confirmation existing-question result is 16 of 30.","why":"A sample of returned passages cannot count helpful statements A or B never returned, establish article-level coverage, or verify source context and attribution. Thirty selected passages are also not a validated estimate for every company or future input.","what_would_settle_it":"For a preselected set of complete articles and their available questions, independently enumerate helpful source matters before seeing outputs, then compare both returned and missing matters through the real writer."}
{"target":"poc/data/collision-v4-probe-2026-11-05-r2/results.json; split-v2-design.md §3","class":"Review probe is narrow and partially unstable","severity":"serious","evidence":"The design cites 13 selected pairs sent three times, including Fed and acquisition pairs merged only 2 of 3 times, and says all eight selected must-stay-apart pairs stayed apart. The prompt and examples were revised using these same cases.","why":"The eight pairs do not establish a general false-merge rate, and the two observed 2-of-3 results show material instability. Expanding review to any probability also has not been evaluated for all additional peers or for Jev cases labelled different and not named closest.","what_would_settle_it":"Replay all stored candidate-to-question pairs with independent labels, including different-labelled peers, and compare false merges and missed duplicates on a held-out set."}
{"target":"tests/test_unkeyed_collision_matching.py; docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/test_split_v2.py","class":"Tests verify routing and shape rather than correctness","severity":"serious","evidence":"The low-probability peer tests use a scripted judge that returns same_question; the D197 test checks only generated key fields; the quote test checks returned source text and one changed number. None checks the actual review’s semantic verdict, distinct quantities sharing a key, or whether a repaired quote still supports its summary.","why":"These tests can pass while the real reviewer merges a distinct question, a broad key merges different quantities, or a repaired quote leaves an unsupported summary. The stated mutants establish selected mechanics only.","what_would_settle_it":"Replay stored decisions through the real writer with independently labelled counterexamples for each contract, and mutate the relevant routing or repair to show each check detects its named defect."}
{"target":"docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split-v2-predictions.md; split-v2-design.md §§4–6","class":"Failed predictions followed by another tuned run","severity":"serious","evidence":"The first v2 run achieved 9 of 20 numeric ladders against a prediction of at least 60% and opened the acquisition again. The final run produced 13 questions against 15–25 and five quote refusals against at most two. The quarterly sentence and quote handling were then changed before the confirmation run.","why":"These are sequential modifications selected using the same first 100 rows, not three independent draws of one final configuration. The confirmation result cannot by itself establish stable improvement or satisfy the phase’s recorded failed-prediction stop rule.","what_would_settle_it":"Freeze the final requests and checks, then use predeclared held-out inputs and a same-day control with the required repeated draws; report every draw and regression."}
{"target":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/plan-v0.md; split-v2-design.md §6; traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1280.json","class":"Production cost and latency not established","severity":"serious","evidence":"The plan says sequential processing is “about 20 s an item measured”; the design reports about $0.6 for one first-100 run. The A probe at seq 1280 used 108,962 input tokens for one request. The experiment starts from a 68-question historical library, and the seven-day run remains stopped.","why":"One short run does not establish latency, spend or failure behavior as the library and evidence grow, including repeated preparation after same-day writes. The estimate also does not prove the service follows this research runner’s path.","what_would_settle_it":"Measure request sizes, per-step cost and elapsed time on stored inputs across small and growing libraries, including actual retry and restart paths, and compare them with an explicit production budget."}
{"summary":{"findings":18}}